#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520528} :activity/id #uuid "df60b178-1cb5-516a-821d-f1274833f6f6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048786 :topic/id #uuid "bea5c820-dd0e-5c3b-936d-578694b34907" :topic/math-academy-id 4171} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259e5a02-0c0f-55f3-bd6c-0425cc1b2fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278926} {:answer/id #uuid "5764bf31-2e34-5139-b5af-336d908fb12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279392} {:answer/id #uuid "b8015639-eca8-56a2-ba22-50eb0b6beb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280209} {:answer/id #uuid "cceb0541-bef3-574b-9eaf-de3916b0c09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280395} {:answer/id #uuid "f640ccfa-97c4-5abd-9713-fdd182bc2d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278926} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d25be3-23d4-5ea5-b1b7-1033574afeb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27445c9b-7e4a-5e28-a2d1-3fc7a72547b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163089" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=2{e}^{x-y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax-2y+b{x}^{2}+cxy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =2, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+2\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+2x-2y+1{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=2+1+(-2)=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1351f0da-3665-50fe-aa04-9f12a6f4264c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520545 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7d3174-3459-579a-b643-b960a7dd075c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520008} {:answer/id #uuid "53608405-55af-52ab-8bb1-8ba8739d1150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520117} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9e8b65-e8ee-589b-9251-9db47e2725f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520136} {:answer/id #uuid "8572f2aa-9106-5587-bca8-e37536bbe74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520184} {:answer/id #uuid "cd348c35-901b-57a1-93a1-95ed5535c117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520117} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0946b1-502a-5aba-b006-539568a4bc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "983968fc-15f6-5756-ab92-7399b778fa93" :question/math-academy-id "q-163108" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{e}^{y}),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax+by-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+cxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{y}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+{e}^{y}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{x{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x+1y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-1)xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "495367cd-f07e-5132-8f32-4e563019fabe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f322457-25fd-5a66-86e8-083d4272a096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516172} {:answer/id #uuid "45a5c347-378b-5252-8c5b-08cec2880c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516254} {:answer/id #uuid "4a73e654-74cd-553c-bd37-5755a05e3bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516264} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9a04e1-a076-5063-b000-58d0bd6211f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516360} {:answer/id #uuid "a5683896-77cb-5f32-b1ff-4d08b73452cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516264} :answer-field/id #uuid "70dfe391-1be5-5314-819b-7419c185891e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8fd8fac-7611-58e8-a16a-eb7f95407000" :question/math-academy-id "q-163132" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y),\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 3 \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+0+x+x+(-9)+3=2x-5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "92a16d7f-ef9b-52e3-b0ea-6bfb381ddffb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213c31e4-604c-5637-a050-4eaeb6c0f751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520038} {:answer/id #uuid "878b145b-533e-56a3-a50a-7a45a1f3deec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520191} {:answer/id #uuid "b5cff024-e8a3-5830-b01f-71c191133afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520247} {:answer/id #uuid "b6db8036-e893-58df-8210-1dab0e50bf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520248} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9dc1c3-ffa1-5373-bb29-a4874096f214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520247} :answer-field/id #uuid "57708cab-0a02-5b87-93ec-6746644b7829" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0b66b9d-4d88-5de4-a9f2-e4468c8b8ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163105" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y+4},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot x+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{32})xy+(-\\frac{1}{64}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{32})+(-\\frac{1}{64})=\\frac{13}{64}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ab8b3d07-995c-53ab-94ae-6bf1bafbde24" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520545}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} {:answer/id #uuid "13106fba-e2d8-590f-9a50-2b1d63582d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281872} {:answer/id #uuid "1abdb628-18f3-5897-af88-0cc7b944c171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281934} {:answer/id #uuid "2a19cffe-821c-5512-84e1-9848e5f50a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282054} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf50d5a-a34b-5ce3-bd0f-8c170f17a78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d02b8fb-22de-53d2-a4aa-1c9d236c7ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af702ba5-d262-51dd-8530-cde831134efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164830" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c41363ef-ff87-5fdf-9c49-497841beb370"}] :activity/title "Second-Degree Taylor Polynomials of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519995}]}
